WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:02.800
Narrator: Can you find
the seal on the ball?

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.800
It's hiding behind those blocks.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.550
What about the rubber band?

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.920
That's over by the alligator.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.600
Does this look familiar?

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.520
These photos are from
the "I Spy" book series,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.450
introduced in 1992, and
there's a good chance

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.000
that either you or your
kids have read them.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.900
But did you know that all of these images

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.160
were created with real objects?

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.500
The magic and mystery within these books

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.733
is directly tied to the imagination of

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:29.710
the photographer behind them,

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:30.543
Walter Wick.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.878
Walter Wick: My name is Walter Wick.

00:00:33.878 --> 00:00:36.460
I am a photo-illustrator,
and I've been doing

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.910
that for I don't know
how long, since 1991.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.360
Narrator: Before "I Spy,"
Walter was a commercial

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.110
photographer in New York City.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.570
One of his clients was Games magazine,

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.260
which created puzzles and
activities for each issue.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.140
But how did Walter end
up working on "I Spy"?

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.710
One day around 1980, Walter
was cleaning up his studio

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.320
and he decided to take a
picture of the stray nuts

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.070
and bolts he had lying around.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.600
Walter used that photo
to promote his work,

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.390
which is how the author
Jean Marzollo saw it.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.970
Walter: I got a call from
Jean Marzollo to do a poster

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.520
for her magazine, which
is called Let's Find Out.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.310
It's a kindergarten magazine.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.990
Narrator: An editor at Scholastic Books

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.460
saw that poster and
asked if Jean and Walter

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.950
wanted to make a
search-and-find children's book.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.110
Jean would write the rhymes,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.187
and Walter would create the images.

00:01:24.187 --> 00:01:26.787
Walter: And I said sure,
and that's how it started.

00:01:26.787 --> 00:01:28.360
Narrator: But perhaps what made the books

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.720
so successful was how
realistic they looked.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.600
The combination of props, sets,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.980
and lighting created an immersive scene.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.470
Originally, Walter used
a large-format camera

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.960
to photograph each scene.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.610
This created huge 8-by-10 negatives.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.280
Which was ideal, because
the larger image size

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.670
captured a ton of detail.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.670
Then around 2004, Walter
switched to digital.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.000
He now uses a Canon 5D.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.500
But Walter was quick to point out

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.010
that the camera does
little of the real work.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.180
Because it just sits there

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.250
while Walter spends
weeks building the sets.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.660
Walter's studio is filled
with the props and materials

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.070
he uses to build these incredible images,

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:10.600
like blocks, buttons,
toys, and even pine cones.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.340
Walter: I would hide the objects
as I would build the shot.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.260
I wouldn't really think about
hiding the objects first.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.820
I would create the shot, make
a list of the hidden objects,

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.540
send it to Jean, and then
she would write the rhyme.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.860
Narrator: Each scene is
arranged very carefully

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.110
around a central idea or theme,

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.840
like nature, antiques, or school.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.130
Walter: One of the problems
with photo illustrations

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:38.150
is you're limited by
what you have available.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.270
The higher-level concepts
became harder and harder

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.740
and harder to do, though, as I went on,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.420
because I didn't want to repeat myself.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.170
Narrator: But "I Spy"
isn't the only series

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.370
Walter has worked on.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.730
He's also the author and photo-illustrator

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.080
of the "Can You See What I
See?" books and "Hey, Seymour!"

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.180
Which follow the search-and-find
style of "I Spy."

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.230
In order to make these scenes a reality,

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:03.680
Walter works with assistants,
fabricators, and model-makers.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.730
For "Can You See What I
See?: Toyland Express,"

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.500
Walter's team built a
toy train from scratch

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.630
because they needed to show it

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.920
being assembled throughout the story.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.420
Each photo can take
anywhere from a couple days

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.430
to several weeks to create.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.050
The attention to detail is part

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.150
of what makes these photos so compelling.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.710
And Walter continues to capture

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.580
the imagination of readers today.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.480
His newest book, "A Ray of Light,"

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.780
is a science book that
uses beautiful photos

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.860
to illustrate the properties of light.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.520
It's a companion to Walter's
1997 book "A Drop of Water."

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.770
Walter: I've always wanted
to do the book on light.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.140
I was a little afraid of it.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:41.850
Because as much as I work
with light and I know light,

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.320
I was a little afraid to
try and to explain the inner

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.330
workings of it to young readers.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.390
But I finally did that.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.428
Narrator: The "I Spy" and
"Can You See What I See?"

00:03:52.428 --> 00:03:56.090
books have over 45
million copies in print.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.490
The care and attention to
detail put into every frame

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.680
still resonates with readers today.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.721
Walter: I was very surprised
from the very beginning

00:04:04.721 --> 00:04:09.246
how intensely kids
looked at my photographs,

00:04:09.246 --> 00:04:12.870
so I just put that same
intensity back into making them.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.320
Narrator: Imagination and the sense

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.453
of discovery are timeless.

